WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.708
SPEAKER 1: Howdy.

00:00:00.708 --> 00:00:01.440
Quick question.

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.830
Does Dialogflow CX integrate
with telephone providers?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.330
SPEAKER 2: Yes, it does.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.880
SPEAKER 1: Can I learn more
about the IVR features?

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.860
SPEAKER 2: Sure, then
stay tuned to learn more.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.332 --> 00:00:20.030
SPEAKER 1: For a full
voice experience,

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.910
your Dialogflow CX
agent can be integrated

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.040
with various
conversation platforms

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.510
such as Avaya, Genesys, Mitel,
Five9, Vonage, and more.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.260
These providers offer a
user interface for callers

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.650
and pass their questions
through Dialogflow's API.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.470
It's important to note that
every platform's integration

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:42.630
handles interactions in
their own unique way.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.350
So depending on the
telephony provider you use,

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.930
you will need to review
their specific documentation.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.150
So let's take a tour.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.030
When we dive into
Dialogflow CX's console,

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.670
on the left side we
click the Manage tab

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.510
and scroll down to the
section called Integrations.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.880
Under the One-click
Telephony header,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.910
you will find partner built-in
telephony integrations

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.070
that were created
by Google partners

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.560
in collaboration with Google.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.120
The following
partners are listed

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.200
as of the making of this video,
which are Avaya and AudioCodes.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.210
There are also
independent integrations

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.690
that are not built in that
are created by organizations

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.250
and individuals.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.030
They are built using
the Dialogflow API.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.920
And as a friendly
heads up, when you

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.680
need support for any
telephony integration,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.850
the partners are the folks
that will assist you.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.370
With that being said,
the overall process

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:40.000
will be to build your
Google Cloud project,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.980
enable the Dialogflow
API within your project,

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.730
and then create a
Dialogflow CX agent.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:50.600
Since most agent conversations
are built within many flows

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.870
and pages in mind, you
will also typically build

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.930
a webhook service to
generate a dynamic response

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:00.050
based on your business' logic.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:01.760
You will also most
likely need to keep

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.130
in mind certain
pieces of information

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.030
for the integration, such as
your project ID, agent ID,

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:09.889
and/or the API key.

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:13.280
IVR settings can be found
at three different levels

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:14.810
in Dialogflow--

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.040
at the agent level, flow
level, and page level.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.130
For starters, you must first
enable the Agent Level Settings

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.670
and select the
Speech and IVR tab.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.620
Then, under the
Advanced settings,

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.490
toggle the Enable
Advanced Settings on.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.650
There are three
features to note.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.360
The first is called end of
speech sensitivity, which

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.230
helps determine how sensitive
the speech end pointing should

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.480
be when a caller
finishes speaking.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.540
And it uses Google Cloud speech.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:50.540
Next is barge-in, which
allows the speaker

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.370
to interrupt the agent.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.080
This is helpful
so that the caller

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.560
doesn't have to wait to hear
the full response from the agent

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:58.820
in order to speak.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.950
Or another way of
looking at it is,

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.840
if you disable the
barge-in setting,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.840
the agent would only begin to
listen to a caller's speech

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.620
until it has completely
finished playing its response.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.740
And last is the Google
Cloud Storage URI,

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:16.220
which enables you to reference
a Google Cloud Storage path back

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.850
in order to access
pre-recorded audio.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:23.420
After enabling IVR settings
at the agent level,

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.180
you can also customize
IVR settings further

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.040
on a per flow level.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.860
You can do this by hovering
over a flow's name,

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.520
click the dots icon, and
choose Flow Settings.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.520
Then you toggle Enable
Advanced Speech Settings.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.340
By enabling IVR features
at the flow level,

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.500
they override the
agent level IVR

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.360
settings it was defaulting to.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.810
This is extremely useful if
you want to have different IVR

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:51.030
settings in a multiflow agent.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.740
The third level of IVR
settings resides on each page.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.820
You can access these by hovering
over a page, clicking the dots,

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.920
then selecting Page Settings.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.830
And same as a flow,
a page's IVR settings

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:07.220
will default to the agent's
settings or flow settings

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.760
unless you specifically select
Customize at the page level.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.550
Now, you may be
thinking, when might

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:17.540
I want to use the page
and flow level settings?

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.920
This depends on your use case.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.570
For example, let's say as
a user dictates and pauses

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:28.870
in between numbers such as, my
account number is 123 [PAUSE]

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.200
45 [PAUSE] 6789.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.300
The user benefits from
extending the timeout,

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.820
so you may want to make sure
that the timeout setting isn't

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.370
too short or the user
will be cut off, which

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.080
is very rude in conversations.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.260
Furthermore, if I stayed
silent because I didn't

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.650
know my account number
and I was looking for it,

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.680
then tweaking the
no-speech timeout setting

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.070
will allow the agent to help
me by further prompting me

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.740
on where to get
that account number.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:01.760
And there you have it, a quick
walk through of IVR features

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.460
and settings in Dialogflow CX.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.950
I left some helpful links
in this video's description

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.780
if you would like to
learn more or if you

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.900
missed any of our previous
videos on Dialogflow CX.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:15.260
I hope to see you in the next
episode of Deconstructing Chat

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:15.830
Bots.

